Goals:
-----

 - Develop a treatment of CPU and GPU versions of the same code that:
 
     1. Allows maintenance of a single code base in a shared Rp 
     namespace 


     2. Avoid the need to create different parallel sets of classes
     in distinct Rpc and Rpg namespaces. 

     3. Create a manageable system that allows for maintaining object
     files and unit tests for several compiled versions of the code 
     simultaneously.

Comments: Goal 1 has been largely accomplished b current system of class
templates in Rp, with "Types" classes as template parameters. This file
proposes a way to accomplish goal 2, and avoid the need for distinct
Rpc and Rpg namespaces.  

Discussion
----------

The most basic problem in maintaining CPU and GPU versions of the same
code arises from the need to create CPU and GPU versions of the classes
that store fields that are defined on the nodes of a mesh, and distinct 
versions of functions that manipulate these fields. All or almost all 
of the data that is stored on the GPU in the current cuda version are
fields of this type.  Relevant mathematical operations include things 
like binary and in-place (compound) arithmetic operations, exponentiation, 
and FFTs.

Strategies for specializing the behavior for CPUs and GPUs can choose
between possible behaviors either at run time (via inheritance and 
polymorphic virtual functions) or at compile time (via templates).

A. Run time polymorphism via inheritance and virtual functions:

Create an interface / base class for each field class and subclasses 
designed to use CPU and GPU memory.  This must rely on the use of virtual 
functions to distinguish behavior, and so requires that any mathematical 
operation that is performed on a field be implemented by a virtual 
functions.

Comments:

   - In virtual functions that implement operations for which the output
     is a field, and one or more other fields may be provided as input
     parameters, it is natural to call the member function on the field 
     that will hold the output, since there is always one output, but
     there may be any number of inputs.

   - To create generic code, functions that take fields as parameters
     must take instances of the base class. Operations are actually only
     allowed for input and output fields that use the same backend.
     There must be a run-time check of some sort of label to ensure that 
     all fields involved in an operation use the same backend. Fields
     in such an operation also must have the same mesh dimension, so it
     is natural to check both of these conditions together.

B. Compile time polymorphism via templates.

This is the proposal that is examined in detail in the remainder of this
file. In this proposal, we define class templates that take some sort of
template parameter that identifies the backend. Specifically, to take
advantage of analogies with the existing implementation, the proposal
is to define many classes that each take two template parameters: 1) The 
integer dimension of space D=1,2, or 3, and 2) A class or enumeration 
that is uniquely identified with a particular backend. I propose to use 
a classes to identify the backend because a classes can contain additional 
information, e.g., about associated used by the associated FFT library for 
real and complex types (which are different for FFTW and for cuFFT).

In this approach, we would define a base class template for RField as

template <int D, class T> 
class Template {};

and then define partial specializations in which T is taken to be 
either T=CpuTypes for CPU code or T=CudaTypes for GPU code written in 
Cuda. 

Class templates with the same two template parameters would also be 
written for a variety of other higher level classes that own or interact
with field classes. A class template Parent<D,T> for a parent class that
owns a field can then have a member Field<D,T>. In this manner we can
create a hierarchy of templates that own each other, such that all of
the templates used in a given executable would have the same values for
both D and T.

Comments:

  - Mathematical operations can be implemented as global functions
    (for vector operations and reduction operations) or as member 
    functions of an associated FFT<D,T> class for FFT operations, 
    exactly as done currently. It appears that no changes would need 
    to be made to any of the existing functions, and that it would 
    remain easy to add new mathematical operations without adding 
    additional virtual functions to the field classes whenever you 
    find it convenient to define a new mathematical operation. 

  - The check that all fields or arrays involved in a mathematical 
    operations must use the same backend would be naturally imposed by 
    the C++ type system, rather than as additional run-time-checks.
    The data types for fields are specializations of templates in
    which one of the template parmeters identifies the backend, so
    the backend is embedded in the type definitions. Functions would
    we defined for allowed operations, involving fields that use the
    same backed, but not for prohibited operations, and the compiler
    will choose the correct version of overloaded functions.

  - I need to think about how to implement unit tests, but it may
    be natural to define some of these as member function templates
    that take the backend types class T as a template argument. 

Thoughts:

  - I think the template approach is actually a rather a natural 
    conceptual fit to a problem, but more awkard to implement in
    C++ because of pecularities of the language. In practice, a
    single backend will always be used throughout execution of 
    a given program, and could be chosen simply be calling a 
    different variant of the executable. We don't need the 
    run-time flexibility provided by run-time polymorphism. 

  - The strongest argument for preferring compile-time polymorhpisms 
    is really that the C++ mechanisms for working with templates in
    a large framework are simply much clumsier than mechanisms for 
    working with a system based on inheritances. The awkwardness of 
    patterns of file inclusion for C++ templates is really a defect 
    of the language that is a result of its descent from C, and 
    the use of the C model for importing modules in which 
    information about other classes is imported by a text preprocessor 
    that literally pastes header files into other files.  The 
    resulting awkardness of large template-based systems in C++ 
    is real, however, and can provide good reasons to favor 
    run-time polymorphisms even for problems in which compile-time 
    polymorphism via templates might otherwise seem like a natural 
    choice. 

  - The current version of PSCF uses a system of templates that
    is in some ways very similar to that proposed here, in which
    all of the classes defined in the Rp name space already have
    two class templates analogous to the two proposed here. The
    template based proposal would allow re-use of all of the 
    existing code for basic mathematical operations, without
    modification, and provides a path to modify class templates
    to improve the design without ever breaking the code. 

  - The template-based system proposed here might or might not 
    be the best option if we were designing a new package from
    scratch - I can see argument for the inheritance based system.
    The template-based design does, however, provide a far easier
    path for improving the design of the current code without
    starting over, because of the formal similarity to the current
    system. Both systems involve class templates with two parameters,
    and simply change the nature of the Types class in a manner
    that can be applied to one class at at time.

  - I can see two options for the nature of the second template
    parameter:

      A. It can be a class, as described above. One advantage of 
      this is that this provides a simple syntax for accessing
      associated information about associated types or other 
      info, via "typename T::Complex" or "T:: backend", etc.

      B. The alternative is to make the second parameter an 
      enum value (type Backend) and then use a Traits class 
      Traits<T>::Complex to access related information. 

    Either would work fine. The main reason that I prefer A is 
    that it is more similar to the current system and provides 
    an easier path for conversion (discussed below) by a method
    that preserves working, testable code at intermediate steps.
    After finishing a conversion to design A, it would be 
    relatively easy to convert to design B in a separate step,
    by globally converting "T::" to "Traits<T>::", if we thought 
    the additional change was worth the trouble.

Outline (order of operations)
-----------------------------

Non-disruptive preparatory changes to existing code design:

1)  Change classes in Rp that use several class template parameters so as 
    to use a single Types class template parameter (essential for later 
    transforming the role of the Types class).

2)  Define a enumeration that identifies backends and make a value of it
    a static member of the existing Types classes. This would allow code 
    that takes a Types class as a template argument to ask explicitly at
    run time what type of backend is being used, and construct logic
    with different paths for different back ends.

3)  Add Complex and Real type aliases to these classes, to represent
    the data types used by the associated FFT classes. Convert use of
    cudaComplex and cudaReal to typename T::Complex and typename T::Real
    where possible in templates in the Rp namespace.
 
4)  Make RField, RFieldDft and CField subclasses of specializations of
    a class DRArray that can have countable references. This will make  
    it is possible to create a zero-cost reference to a CPU field, and
    increase the similarity of CPU and GPU code (since references are
    already possible for a DeviceArray).

5)  Create aliases named HostRField, HostRFieldDft and HostCField in 
    the Types classes. In the CPU version, make these specializations
    of DRArray, so that they can be used as references.

6)  Develop an abstraction to treat device <-> copy operations that are
    required for some Gpu implementations but not for the analogous CPU 
    implementations. in order to further reduce the number of functions 
    that need different implementations for Cpu and Gpu code. The CPU
    implementations should create a reference rather than a copy, and
    the GPU implementations should copy data between host and device.

7)  More generally, reduce the number of class templates in Rp which
    require subclasses that need to implement virtual functions in Rpc 
    and Rpg namespaces.  The less inheritance is used to specialize in 
    the existing code, the easier it will be to transform to a purely 
    template based design.

8)  Change names of any remaining class templates in the Rp namespace 
    that require explicit specialization of subclasses in the Rpc and 
    Rpg namespaces by adding name "Base" to the base class template 
    name.  Distinguishing names of base and derived classes will prevent 
    later clashes between base and derived class templates after we get 
    rid of the Rpc and Rpg namespaces, and define both base and
    derived classes in the Rp namespace. 

9)  Define preprocessor macros for explicit instantiation declarations
    and definitions, to reduce boilerplate and simplify the conversion 
    to a new system. These can be named:

       PSCF_TMPL_DT_DECLARE(Class, Types),
       PSCF_TMPL_DT_DEFINE(Class, Typtes),

Disruptive changes:

1)  Create Pscf::CpuTypes by copying Pscf::Rpc::Types and create 
    Pscf::CudaTypes by copying Pscf::Rpg::Types.

    At this stage, these two new classes in Pscf will include forward 
    declarations of classes in Rpc and Rpg and aliases for classes 
    defined in these namespaces. This is intended to be a temporary
    state - the process described will allow us to later remove all
    of these references to program-level namespaces.  Because the 
    existing Types class header files contains only forward 
    declarations and aliases, but don't include other header files
    from Rpc or Rpg, these header should be usuable anywhere in Pscf.

2)  Change references to Rpc::Types to CpuTypes and Rpg::Types to 
    CudaTypes throughout Rpc and Rpg namespaces, respectively. Also
    change file names for the relevant header file includes.

    (All code should compile again at this point. Changes have thus 
     far been superficial changes in class names, namespaces in which
     they are defined, and header file locations).

3)  Create templates for RField, RFieldDft, CField etc. in Prdc

    - Create empty base class templates in Prdc of the form
      class RField<int D, class T> where T is a "*Types" class,
      and similarly for RFieldDft, CField, FFT, etc.

    - Make explicit partial specializations of RField, 
      RFieldDft, CField, FFT, etc. that are just renamed versions of 
      existing classes now in Prdc::Cpu and Prdc::Cuda. Each of these
      partial specializations will still have D as an unspecified
      template parameter, like the existing classes.

      For example: 

         Prdc::Cpu::RField<int D> => Prdc::RField<int D, CpuTypes>
 
      Idea: Use template parameter rather than Cpu and Cuda namespace 
      qualifiers to distinguish names of Cpu and Gpu variants of 
      analogous field and FFT classes.  

    - Advantage: Template parameters in the class template of a 
      parent object can be used in type definitions for member
      variables and function interfaces.  Note: Namespaces cannot 
      be used as template parameters, so we have to use a class or
      enumeration as a template parameter to identify a backend.

    - Note: No change should be necessary to functions defined in 
      VecOp and Reduce. These functions operate on DArray or 
      DeviceArray containers, which are base classes for the CPU 
      and GPU field containers, respectively.

4)  Make any other required changes in Prdc namespace to get this 
    namespace level directory to compile. Look for direct reference 
    to, e.g., RField<D> and FFT<D> template specializations and 
    replace these by RField<D,T> and FFT<D,T> etc.

5)  Repeat change in class template names for definitions of all 
    prdc unit tests. At this point code and unit tests in src/prdc 
    should compiles and run successfully. 

6)  Change aliases to Fields and FFT types in CpuTypes and CudaTypes. 
    This will immediately allow re-use throughout the class templates 
    in Rp.

7)  Find  and replace direct references to, e.g., RField<D> and FFT<D> 
    type names in Rpc and Rpg namespaces with Field<D,CpuTypes> and
    FFT<D,CpuTypes>, etc.

    (Code in rpc and rpg should compiles again at this point, but not 
    unit tests)

8)  Find and replace direct references to RField<D>, FFT<D>, etc. 
    in rpc and rpg unit tests with, references to Field<D,CpuTypes> ,
    FFT<D,CpuTypes>, etc.

    (All code and unit tests should again compile and run)

9)  In class templates in the Rp namespace, globally change references 
    via "typename T::RField" etc to more explicit RField<T,D> within 
    class templates in the Rp namespace. This should cause no change 
    in behavior, and is a first step towards removing need for a long 
    list of aliases in the Types classes.

11) Go through the following operation for class templates defined in Rp
    for which aliases exist in the Types classes, class by class:

    For each class Rp::Class :

       - In rpc/ and rpg/ directories, remove definitions of subclass 
         templates Rpc::Class<D> and Rpg::Class<D> for each class, and
         instead explicitly instantiation Rp::Class<D, CpuTypes> and 
         Rp::Class<D, GpuTypes>. For the moment, the required explicit 
         instantiation declarations and definitions can be done within 
         existing files in the rpc/ and rpg/ directories, by just 
         changing class template, Types and namespace names. 

         Note: It appears that the removal of definitions of subclasses 
         needs to be done manually. This is the main reason that there
         will need to be some work done manually, class-by-class. Use of 
         macros for explicit instantiation declarations and definitions
         should simplify other aspects of the conversion.

       - After each such conversion of a subclass to an explicitly
         instantiated template specialization, change the corresponding 
         alias in the corresponding Types class. (Everything should 
         compile again).

       - Replaced all references in Rp namespace via "typename T::Class" 
         to Class<D, T> in existing templates in the Rp namespace, then 
         remove the associated alias from the Types class. (Everything 
         should still compile).

       - Replace references to Class<D> to, e.g., Class<D,CpuTypes> in 
         unit tests.

    At the end of this process, the Types classes will contain almost 
    no aliases, except those for Real and Complex types used by FFT 
    library and any others that we intend to preserve in the new system. 
    Each Types classes will have been gradually transformed from a 
    container for a long lists of aliases for classes defined in Rpc or 
    Rpg into much simpler identifier for a backend, with a limited amount
    of type information for types used by the FFT class.

12) For each class that is defined in Rpc or Rpg but for which no alias
    is defined in the Types class, repeat the process applied above for
    classes that are referred to via aliases defined in the Types classes.
    The remaining classes will primarily be classes that implement
    specific algorithms (bd steps, mc moves, compressors, analyzers, etc), 
    which are subclasses of base classes for which an aliases is defined
    in the current Types class. 

    At the end of this process, their will not be any classes defined 
    in the Rpc or Rpg namespaces, and these namespaces will cease to
    exist.

13) At this point, there will still be distinct headers in the rpc/ and
    rpg/ namespaces for each class. The next step will be to merge these
    headers into headers defined in the rp/ namespace, using macros to
    place all required explicit instantiation declarations in the main
    header. The macro used for explicit instantiation declarations 
    within headers in the rp/ directory can rely on preprocessor logic
    to create declarations only for backends that have been enabled by
    the build system.

    At this point, the rpc/ and rpg/ namespaces will contain *.cpp or
    *. cu files, respectively, but no headers. Each *.cpp file or *.cu
    file will contain just a set of explicit instantiation directives,
    all of which can be instantiated using one or two macros.  The rpc/ 
    and rpg/ namespaces will thus essentially have been converted into 
    separate build directories for CPU and GPU code, to allow both 
    versions to be built simultaneously. The bld/ directory can then
    have rpc/ and rpg/ directories, but no rp/ directory, as in the
    current system. 

